WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.250
CARTER MORGAN: In
2018, there were

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:06.180
over 16,000 vulnerabilities
published in public databases.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.490
And that's just the
ones we know about.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.740
But a program as simple
as Add A Cat To That

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.500
couldn't possibly have
one of those, right?

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:14.927
Right?

00:00:14.927 --> 00:00:15.510
You know what?

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:16.079
Stick around.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:18.150
Because we should probably
use Cloud Registry

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:21.470
Scanner to check on our software
supply chain, just in case.

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.520
Add A Cat To That is
a very short program,

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.110
no more than a
dozen lines of code

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.180
implement its core
functionality.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:28.980
But believe it or
not, it has hundreds

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.910
of dependencies, each of which
expands our attack surface.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.600
Using other people's
work made Add A Cat

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.400
To That easy to write, but
it's also a potential weakness.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.968
Any one of those libraries could
be neglected or compromised.

00:00:41.968 --> 00:00:44.010
And with so many of them,
it's next to impossible

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.050
to manually keep up with
the ever-growing list

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.670
of vulnerabilities.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.240
What's a developer to do?

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.570
Fortunately, we're using
Google Container Registry

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.203
to store our container images.

00:00:53.203 --> 00:00:55.620
It has a feature that can help
us secure our supply chain,

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.930
called Vulnerability Scanning.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.580
Once we've enabled
this feature, we

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.110
can go back to
our list of images

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.060
and look under the
Vulnerabilities column.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.920
And we can see that
our tiny little image

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.100
has over 600 vulnerabilities
and only 38 fixes.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.100
We could dive into each
of these vulnerabilities

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.670
to see if we could fix them.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:15.300
For example, let's click on
one of the ImageMagick issues

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.510
to find details on exactly
what the vulnerability is--

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.620
"an uninitialized variable
leading to memory corruption."

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.370
Now we could dig through
the variables use

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:25.985
cases to see if this
error is relevant to us.

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:27.360
And even if there's
not a fix, we

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.550
might be able to set up
some secondary measures

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:31.120
to protect ourselves.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.022
But let's try a different way.

00:01:34.022 --> 00:01:35.730
Looking through the
vulnerabilities list,

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:39.150
we notice that a lot of them
are related to ImageMagick.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.770
So let's reduce
our attack surface

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.530
by swapping out ImageMagick
with the simpler Python

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.110
library, called Pillow.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.120
We can also improve
our security posture

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.280
by choosing a
different base image.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.500
We're currently
using Python 3.7,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.077
which contains a lot of
dependencies we don't need.

00:01:55.077 --> 00:01:56.910
And many of the
vulnerabilities we're seeing

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:58.560
are coming from that image.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.050
We can remove most of them by
switching to a slimmer base

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.410
image, such as Alpine Linux.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:05.027
And since we're
using Alpine, we'll

00:02:05.027 --> 00:02:07.110
have to change our docker
file to manually include

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.910
the specific libraries we need.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:12.540
But other than that, the docker
file is pretty much the same.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.880
With just these two
fixes, we're down to zero

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.070
known vulnerabilities.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.200
Google Container Registry's
vulnerability scanning

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.480
showed us how to
improve our security.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.390
But those changes
came with a cost.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.790
Because we switched from
ImageMagick to Pillow,

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.580
the app is slower and uses
more memory than before.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.880
But for us, this was a trade-off
we were willing to make.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.290
Eliminating over 600
bugs in a few minutes?

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:36.930
Yeah, that seems like a win.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.770
Add A Cat To That
has to be secure.

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:41.520
Good thing we found
out about these issues

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.300
before we had an incident.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.440
But I vaguely remember
making a last minute

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.552
check-in last night.

00:02:48.552 --> 00:02:50.010
Hm.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:51.108
I'm Carter Morgan.

00:02:51.108 --> 00:02:53.400
And in the next video, we'll
use Cloud Security Scanner

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.310
to check for any silly mistakes
that might have been introduced

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:59.280
into the code base by someone--

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.930
I don't know who--

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:01.530
gremlins.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.280
[MUSIC PLAYING]

